                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "marking as resolved since https://github.com/openbmc/docs/blob/643e73605907251d80166d3d3c984456fc7fd599/CONTRIBUTING.md?plain=1#L487\nsays we don't need it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "nit: what about `getStringFromObj`  which is a bit shorter."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Is saving 3 characters really important?  If i wanted to save characters, i'd do something like:\n\nstrFromObject(...)"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "it's not important to me, we can close this comment."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Did you consider to use a structured binding declaration, to return a tuple here and then use it like this later:\n\n```\nauto [name, foundName] = getStringFromObject(obj, \"Name\");\nif (!foundName){\n    return;\n}\n\n// do any mutation on the `name` variable here in case of escaping for DBus, template var replacement or whatever\n```\n\nIn that case we avoid having to copy it to an `std::string` later if we need to pass a `std::string&` to another function.\n\nWe also avoid dealing with raw pointers. I would like to avoid raw pointers if possible since they could spread throughout the codebase and then we would have to do `nullptr` checks."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "foundName would be a bool?  If I were to follow that pattern, I'd do std::expected<std::string, bool> but returning nullptr on not found seems pretty standard.\n\nKeep in mind, your example doesn't work for the non-const cast because you put auto instead of auto&."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> If I were to follow that pattern, I'd do std::expected<std::string, bool> \n\nsounds good too. There are various solutions without using raw pointer.\n\n> Keep in mind, your example doesn't work for the non-const cast because you put auto instead of auto&.\n\nI don't intend to propose a complete solution here, you could probably come up with something better / specialize.\n\nMy main point is that we avoid introducing raw pointers if we can avoid it.\n\nBy using the raw pointer we are coupling the valid scope of that pointer to the lifetime of the lohmann json object.\n\nI would prefer if we just copied out the string which we need and avoid those troubles.\n\nIf it's somehow relevant for performance (in a measurable way) and done in code that has decent test coverage, i would be in favor of such optimizations.\n\nSince this is a utility function which can be called anywhere i doubt we can meaningfully cover all the call sites.\n\nCopying out the string seems like a safer alternative to me."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I'd prefer we avoid the copy because this is a generic routine, and copies are known to be \"expensive\".  Also keep in mind that there's non const use cases that need to pull the string out, then modify it, so we can't have an \"always safe\" version of this.  I think it makes the most sense to just return a pointer/reference."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> I'd prefer we avoid the copy because this is a generic routine, and copies are known to be \"expensive\". \n\nYeah but that's an optimization concern. Optimization concerns apply to code which has a good test coverage. (IMO)\n\n> Also keep in mind that there's non const use cases that need to pull the string out, then modify it\n\nYeah i don't think EM should do this. If it does that now, better to refactor that.\n\njson just happens to be our config format, it should be in the config parsing and persistence layer and not all over EM internals (IMO).\n\n> I think it makes the most sense to just return a pointer/reference.\n\nIf it's at all possible, let's go with the reference then to avoid putting nullptr checks everywhere."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> > I'd prefer we avoid the copy because this is a generic routine, and copies are known to be \"expensive\". \n> \n> Yeah but that's an optimization concern. Optimization concerns apply to code which has a good test coverage. (IMO)\n\nWe disagree about the relationship with testing, and that's fine, we don't need to go into it in this review context.\n\n> \n> > Also keep in mind that there's non const use cases that need to pull the string out, then modify it\n> \n> Yeah i don't think EM should do this. If it does that now, better to refactor that.\n\nFor things like \"shouldn't do this\" type concerns we should look in terms of patches or concrete changes.  The cases that I saw were valid.\n\n> \n> json just happens to be our config format, it should be in the config parsing and persistence layer and not all over EM internals (IMO).\n\nSame as above, this is a review for a refactor, there's no need to have a debate about things not in this commit.\n\n> \n> > I think it makes the most sense to just return a pointer/reference.\n> \n> If it's at all possible, let's go with the reference then to avoid putting nullptr checks everywhere.\n\nIf we want to use a reference in c++23, we need to return by\n```\nstd::optional<std::reference_wrapper<T>>\n```\nor\n```\nT*\n```\n\nstd::optional<T&> is only supported in c++26."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> If we want to use a reference in c++23, we need to return by\n\nsorry, i did not know that. In that case a reference wrapper seems like a good choice.\n\nWhen C++26 rolls around we will then be able to simplify again."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I have a minor preference for pointer here, as it's generally more understood and used than reference_wrapper."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/json_utils.cpp",
                    "line": 21,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't want to argue this point forever, and we can change it later. Have it your way i guess."
                },
